"During a multi-homed BGP peering setup with our Huawei NE40E routers, we need to ensure outbound traffic prefers a specific transit provider by assigning a higher local preference value of 200. However, we've noticed some received routes don't carry this attribute. What's the proper way to implement this preference at the router level when processing updates that lack explicit local-preference values?"

To dynamically append the link-bandwidth attribute to unlabeled IPv4 prefixes from adjacent EBGP neighbors, apply the following CLI configuration under the relevant BGP group or neighbor context:

```
configure router bgp group "EBGP_GROUP"
    link-bandwidth
        add-to-received-ebgp ipv4
    exit
exit
```

Replace `"EBGP_GROUP"` with your specific BGP group name. This configuration automatically injects the link-bandwidth community for directly connected EBGP peers without manual prefix handling, ensuring route stability while optimizing peering performance.
