## User question:
"During a recent core network upgrade involving multiple Nokia 7750 SR routers, we encountered a situation where BGP path selection was inadvertently influenced by IGP metrics to next-hop addresses when evaluating identical NLRIs from our primary BGP peers. What's the proper method to modify this default behavior so route comparisons strictly adhere to BGP attributes rather than incorporating the resolved IGP distance?"
---
## Proposed answer:
```console
configure router bgp best-path-selection
    ignore-nh-metric
exit
```

This configuration disables IGP metric consideration during BGP best-path selection, forcing evaluations to rely strictly on BGP attributes. The change applies globally to all routes learned from base router BGP peers.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
ignore-nh-metric
no ignore-nh-metric
FuncDef:
This command instructs BGP to disregard the resolved distance to the BGP next-hop in its decision process for selecting the best route to a destination. When configured in theconfig>router>bgp>best-path-selectioncontext, this command applies to the comparison of two BGP routes with the same NLRI learned from base router BGP peers. When configured in theconfig>service>vprncontext, this command applies to the comparison of two BGP-VPN routes for the same IP prefix imported into the VPRN from the base router BGP instance. When configured in theconfig>service>vprn>bgp>best-path-selectioncontext, this command applies to the comparison of two BGP routes for the same IP prefix learned from VPRN BGP peers.
