## Proposed answer:
To set the TTL value to 128 for EBGP update messages in a multi-hop peering scenario, use the `multihop` command under the relevant BGP group or neighbor configuration. Here's the CLI command:

```
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        multihop 128
    exit
exit
```

Replace `<group-name>` with your EBGP peer group name. If applied to a specific neighbor instead, use:

```
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        neighbor <ip-address>
            multihop 128
        exit
    exit
exit
```

This sets the outgoing BGP packet TTL to 128, ensuring proper traversal through intermediate hops and firewalls with TTL constraints.
