---
## User question:
"During a recent core network upgrade involving multiple Nokia 7750 SR PE routers, we encountered a situation where BGP next-hop resolution was failing for certain VPN-IP routes. What's the proper method to ensure all received VPN-IP routes get automatically imported into a designated dummy VRF specifically maintained for next-hop resolution purposes, without affecting the production routing tables?"
---
## Proposed answer:
```
configure router bgp
    enable-rr-vpn-forwarding
    next-hop-resolution
        labeled-routes
            transport-tunnel
                family vpn
                    resolution-filter ldp rsvp
                exit
            exit
        exit
    exit
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
enable-rr-vpn-forwarding
no enable-rr-vpn-forwarding
FuncDef:
When this command is configured all received VPN-IP routes, regardless of route target, are imported into the dummy VRF, where the BGP next-hops are resolved. Thelabel-route-transport-tunnelunderconfig>router>bgp>next-hop-resolutiondetermines what types of tunnels are eligible to resolve the next-hops. If a received VPN-IP route from IBGP peer X is resolved and selected as best so that it can be re-advertised to an IBGP peer Y, and the BGP next-hop is modified towards peer Y (by using the next-hop-self command in Y s group or neighbor context or by using a next-hop action in an export policy applied to Y) then BGP allocates a new VPRN service label value for the route, signals that new label value to Y and programs the IOM to do the corresponding label swap operation. The supported combinations of X and Y are outlined below:
