During our core router upgrade in New York's primary data center, we need to establish BGP Monitoring Protocol visibility for NYC-DC1 to track peer session health — what's the proper procedure to implement this BMP collector configuration on our Huawei NE40E 7750 platform while maintaining existing production traffic flows?

To modify the BGP next-hop resolution configuration to exclusively use the 'core-routes' policy for evaluating candidate routes, apply the following CLI commands:

```
/configure router bgp next-hop-resolution policy "core-routes"
```

This command configures the BGP next-hop resolution process to only consider routes permitted by the 'core-routes' policy. Routes rejected by the policy will be excluded from next-hop resolution, ensuring stricter control over path selection.
------------
As a network device expert, please determine whether the following answer correctly responds to the user's question based on the router configuration manual. Only output "Correct" or "Incorrect" without any explanation or additional information. You must make your own judgment based on the Router configuration manual provided to you, not on your own knowledge.
---
## User question:
"During a recent core network upgrade, we're implementing stricter control over BGP path selection on our Nokia 7750 SR routers. I need to ensure that only routes matching specific criteria are considered valid next hops—specifically, those permitted by the 'core-routes' policy. What's the proper method to modify the BGP next-hop resolution configuration so it exclusively references this route policy when evaluating candidate routes?"
---
## Proposed answer:
To modify the BGP next-hop resolution configuration to exclusively use the 'core-routes' policy for evaluating candidate routes, apply the following CLI commands:

```
/configure router bgp next-hop-resolution policy "core-routes"
```

This command configures the BGP next-hop resolution process to only consider routes permitted by the 'core-routes' policy. Routes rejected by the policy will be excluded from next-hop resolution, ensuring stricter control over path selection.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
policy <policy-name>
no policy
FuncDef:
This command specifies the name of a policy statement to use with the BGP next-hop resolution process. The policy controls which IP routes in RTM are eligible to resolve the BGP next-hop addresses of IPv4 and IPv6 routes. The policy has no effect on the resolution of BGP next-hops to MPLS tunnels. If a BGP next-hop of an IPv4 or IPv6 route R is resolved in RTM and the longest matching route for the next-hop address is an IP route N that is rejected by the policy then route R is unresolved; if the route N is accepted by the policy then it becomes the resolving route for R.
ParaDef:
{'Parameters': 'policy-name', 'Info': 'Specifies the route policy name. Allowed values are any string up to 64 characters long composed of printable, 7-bit ASCII characters. If the string contains special characters (#, $, spaces, and so on), the entire string must be enclosed within double quotes. Route policies are configured in the config>router>policy-options context.'}
---
